{
  "abstract": "We prove lower bounds for simple critical zeros of the Riemann zeta function on all intervals of length $T^{0.46680}$ and on almost all prescribed intervals whose expected zero count diverges. The latter intervals have simple critical proportion above $2.15\\%$. For some fixed $e>0$, the proportion of starts with no simple critical zero in a window of length $B/\\log T$ is $O_\\varepsilon(B^{-1+\\varepsilon})$ uniformly up to physical length $T^e$. Combining this estimate with a rank witness at bandwidth $0.52168$ gives bounded normalized counting moments of order $1+\\alpha$ for full consecutive simple-critical gaps, for $0<\\alpha<e/(e+0.04336)$. For each fixed derivative order we prove exponential strict-left depth bounds at comparable positive heights. At power lengths $T^\\eta$, the bounds hold at all starts for $\\eta>1/3$ in an explicit rate range, and simultaneously through typical anchors with rate $k$ and exceptional measure $O(T^{1-\\delta})$ whenever $k+\\delta<\\eta<1$. At shorter lengths the rates $0<k<1/4$ hold through typical anchors with exceptional measure $O(T/B)$ at minimum length $B/\\log T$ in the logarithmic range. We also prove a local increasing-convex comparison of the strict-left zeta and first-derivative divisors and evaluate the full finite-depth fourth trace used in the rank bounds.",
